From d4417923196a6417b651ab256f3190eca909493e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 04:08:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] PCI: add Realtek RTL819x PCIe host controller

Add a driver for the PCIe host 1.1 x1 controller of the Realtek
RTL819x MIPS SoCs.  The root port config space is memory mapped;
downstream config space is accessed through an indexed window keyed on
bus<<8 | devfn, implemented in .map_bus so the generic
pci_generic_config_{read,write} accessors work unchanged.

The controller is registered through the generic of_pci host bridge
framework, like pcie-mt7621: devm_pci_alloc_host_bridge() plus
pci_host_probe(), with the bus ranges parsed from the "ranges" property
of the device node.  The driver creates no interrupt domain: root port
and downstream INTx interrupts are resolved by the PCI core from the
interrupt-map of the host bridge device node, which maps all four INTx
pins onto input 21 of the SoC interrupt controller.

The driver powers up the PHY through its MDIO (parameter table selected
by the 25/40 MHz strap), asserts PERST# through the system controller,
waits for LTSSM to reach L0 and then probes the bus.  The PERST#
register and bit differ across the family (RTL8197F: the IP-enable bit
of the system-enable register; the RTL8196E-generation clock-manage bit
is recorded in a comment, not instantiated) and only the RTL8197F
compatible is declared for now.

MACH_REALTEK_RTL819X selects PCI_DRIVERS_GENERIC, so the driver can be
built as a module; the SoC dtsi describes the bus range explicitly
and no I/O window, which the generic framework cannot map (no
remap hook for it).

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
